From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 00:46:51 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] windows.security.enterprisedata: give the async callback the
 argument it is called with.

allowed_async took three arguments where async_operation_callback passes
four, the last saying whether the call is the deferred one.  It worked
only because the callee never looked; the compiler said so on every
build.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 3055df0d734fbd7ff2a49497feab23a9b058ac10)

diff --git a/dlls/windows.security.enterprisedata/protectionpolicy.c b/dlls/windows.security.enterprisedata/protectionpolicy.c
index e83d1299c34..44175a4c22d 100644
--- a/dlls/windows.security.enterprisedata/protectionpolicy.c
+++ b/dlls/windows.security.enterprisedata/protectionpolicy.c
@@ -602,7 +602,7 @@ static const struct IActivationFactoryVtbl policy_factory_vtbl =
 /* Every access question has the same answer while no policy is in force.  The
  * operation is created already complete, so a caller that awaits it is not
  * left waiting on a policy engine that does not exist. */
-static HRESULT allowed_async( IUnknown *invoker, IUnknown *param, PROPVARIANT *result )
+static HRESULT allowed_async( IUnknown *invoker, IUnknown *param, PROPVARIANT *result, BOOL called_async )
 {
     result->vt = VT_I4;
     result->lVal = ProtectionPolicyEvaluationResult_Allowed;
